  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Configuration Setup**\n\nI\'ve examined the `index.tsx` file; it seems standard. The critical piece, however, is the `vite.config.ts`, where the environment variable `process.env.API_KEY` is being defined, specifically using `env.GEMINI_API_KEY`. This is how the API key is getting set for use within the application.\n\n\n**Investigating Key Injection**\n\nOkay, so I realized using `define` in `vite.config.ts` makes `process.env.API_KEY` a static string, not dynamic. I need a way for the browser to dynamically update the API key if a user selects a new key. I\'m wondering if the platform provides `window.process.env.API_KEY` or a similar global variable. The `@google/genai` guidelines say the key is injected automatically via `process.env.API_KEY`, but this doesn\'t clarify HOW the key is injected in a dynamic manner.\n\n\n**Exploring Dynamic Variable Access**\n\nI\'m now investigating how to make the API key dynamic. It seems that if I use `process.env.API_KEY` without `define` in Vite, I\'ll hit a browser error because `process` isn\'t natively defined. However, I\'m now testing whether `(window as any).process?.env?.API_KEY` will provide dynamic access, and if the platform is injecting it into the `window` object. I also found `process.env.API_KEY` used directly in `App.tsx`, and I need to figure out how to make this dynamic rather than static. I\'m checking if `window.aistudio` will provide access.\n\n\n**Confirming Variable Injection**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the API key is injected. The official guidelines state the API key is automatically available as `process.env.API_KEY`, and I think that a shim is needed. It seems the user is getting 403 errors, so that suggests the key is being used, but it\'s the wrong one; if the user selects a new key without a refresh, it is clear that the old key will be used. Also, I need to verify that `openSelectKey()` doesn\'t trigger a full page refresh, but just updates state, so that the old key continues to be used after a new one is selected.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve decided to refine the error handling, as that addresses the immediate need. I\'m focusing on improving the `handleAiError` function to specifically catch 403 errors, as this indicates a permission issue, likely due to an invalid or leaked API key. My goal is to prompt the user for a new key in this scenario. I\'m also ensuring the error message is clear and actionable. I\'m no longer searching for how to change the API key dynamically, as that is a separate issue from this error handling fix, and I should separate concerns.\n\n\n**Improving Error Handling Logic**\n\nMy focus is on refining the error handling logic, specifically addressing the 403 errors. The current logic checks for \"permission\" and \"403\" in the error message. I\'m verifying this correctly catches permission errors, even when `error.message` is undefined, by checking if the nested object exists and if so, taking its message. This should trigger the `window.aistudio.openSelectKey()` call. Also, I\'m checking model names, and verifying that `gemini-3-flash-preview` is the correct model for all tasks. Everything checks out!\n\n\n**Verifying Model and Error**\n\nI\'ve confirmed `gemini-2.5-flash-image` is free, and `gemini-3.1-flash-image-preview` is paid. A 403 error with the free model suggests a leaked or invalid default key. So, prompting for a new key in `handleAiError` still makes sense. I\'m now double-checking that implementation.\n\n\n**Refining Error Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the `handleAiError` function. I realized that very long error messages, particularly those in JSON format, might make the `alert` box too cumbersome. Therefore, I need to truncate the `errorMsg` before displaying it. I\'m also considering adding a recommendation to the user to retry the operation. However, retrying the operation requires capturing and re-executing the original function with its arguments. This is complex. So, for now, I will just give a retry message. Finally, I will display the user\'s error: `AI Error: {\"error\":{\"code\":`.\n\n\n"
